          "title": "Data states, classification, and handling",
          "lead": "Protection follows data through storage, transmission, processing, sharing, retention, and destruction.",
          "keyIdeas": [
            "Data at rest, in transit, and in use",
            "Classification, ownership, and handling rules",
            "Retention, backup, masking, and secure disposal"
          ],
          "content": "Data at rest sits on storage, data in transit moves across a connection, and data in use is being processed. Encryption methods and threats differ across these states. Full-disk encryption helps a lost powered-off laptop but may not protect files from malware running after the user logs in. Classification labels information by sensitivity and business need. The data owner decides classification and access requirements; custodians operate systems according to those decisions.\n\nHandling rules cover storage locations, sharing, printing, backup, retention, and disposal. Collecting less sensitive data reduces both risk and compliance burden. Masking, tokenization, and redaction can limit exposure in testing or reporting. Disposal may require cryptographic erasure, secure overwrite where meaningful, degaussing for suitable media, or physical destruction. Verify the method fits the technology - flash storage and cloud services complicate simple overwrite assumptions.\n\nA simple commercial scheme might use public, internal, confidential, and restricted.\n\nEach level needs concrete handling rules: approved storage, recipients, external sharing, encryption, printing, backup, retention, and destruction. Overclassification makes work difficult; underclassification exposes valuable information. Owners review labels as information ages. Copies multiply through email, collaboration, caches, exports, logs, analytics, and backups. Deleting the original document may not delete those copies. Retention balances operational value, law, contract, investigation holds, and breach impact.\n\nData minimization asks whether the organization needs to collect or keep each field at all.\n\n## Worked case and analyst reasoning\n\nConsider this case: A support export contains personal data in logs and an analyst screenshot. Start with a neutral statement of what was reported, then locate the original records. Do not let the alert title decide the outcome. Identify the subject, asset, action, outcome, time, source, and affected security property. The concepts that frame this chapter are Data at rest, in transit, and in use; Classification, ownership, and handling rules; Retention, backup, masking, and secure disposal.\n\nExplain how each changes the question, the likely impact, or the control boundary.\n\n## Evidence plan.\n\nCollect only what the authorized investigation needs. Relevant evidence includes classification, fields, recipients, storage, retention, redaction and deletion. Record provenance, timestamp and time zone, collection account, source health, retention, and any transformation performed by a parser or enrichment service. Separate direct observation from external reputation, derived data, and analyst inference. If a source is absent, stale, or outside coverage, say so.\n\nNo alert and no log are different conditions.\n\n## Reasoning sequence.\n\nEstablish whether the activity succeeded, whether it was authorized, and how far it reached. Compare the affected entity with its own history, peers, approved changes, and business role. Form a theory that is capable of being disproved. Search first around the known time and entity, then expand using durable relationships such as account, device, process ancestry, session, destination, permission, or data flow.\n\nKeep a query journal so another analyst can reproduce the scope.\n\nA common analytical error is collecting more sensitive data than the case needs. Avoid it by naming what each control actually guarantees and by checking adjacent bypass or recovery paths. Choose containment that matches confidence and consequence. A host isolation, account disablement, firewall block, or data-sharing change can interrupt legitimate work and may destroy transient evidence, so follow the incident plan and obtain the required authority.\n\nDefine how the action will be confirmed and reversed.\n\n## Closure and learning.\n\nValidate the original risk, not merely the absence of a repeated alert. Record verified facts, competing explanations considered, scope, action, user or service impact, remaining uncertainty, and the next owner. If the activity is benign, tune narrowly and preserve coverage. If a weakness contributed, create a tracked corrective action with owner, due date, test, and expiration for any exception. This closes the loop between monitoring, response, engineering, and governance.",
