          "content": "Hardening starts with a supported operating system and an approved baseline. Remove or disable unused services, change defaults, restrict local administrators, configure a host firewall, apply updates, and protect boot and storage. Application allowlisting permits approved software rather than attempting to recognize every malicious program. Traditional anti-malware emphasizes known malicious files. Endpoint detection and response also collects behavior and supports investigation or containment.\n\nNeither replaces patching, least privilege, or backups.\n\nAlerts must be reviewed; an installed agent that has stopped reporting provides false comfort. Mobile devices need screen locks, encryption, updates, trusted application sources, and remote-management policy. Backups should be isolated enough that ransomware cannot rewrite every copy. Test restoration. A “successful backup job” proves data was copied, not that it can be recovered. A hardening baseline specifies allowed services, accounts, settings, logging, software, and update state.\n\nCompare actual state with the baseline and track approved exceptions. Secure Boot checks trusted startup components; TPM-backed protection can guard keys; disk encryption protects storage; screen lock protects unattended sessions. Each addresses a different threat. Patch operating systems, applications, browsers, drivers, and firmware. Test critical updates and maintain rollback, but do not let “testing” become indefinite delay. Remove unsupported software.\n\nEndpoint management should report coverage and agent health: a machine missing from the console may be unmanaged rather than compliant. Validate that hardening preserved the system’s required job.\n\n## Worked case and analyst reasoning\n\nConsider this case: A workstation is fully patched but still runs with local administrator rights and unsafe services. Start with a neutral statement of what was reported, then locate the original records. Do not let the alert title decide the outcome. Identify the subject, asset, action, outcome, time, source, and affected security property. The concepts that frame this chapter are Patching, baselines, and service reduction; Host firewall, anti-malware, EDR, and application control; Secure Boot, disk encryption, screen lock, and backups.\n\nExplain how each changes the question, the likely impact, or the control boundary.\n\n## Evidence plan.\n\nCollect only what the authorized investigation needs. Relevant evidence includes accounts, services, baseline, application allowlist, encryption, firewall and logging. Record provenance, timestamp and time zone, collection account, source health, retention, and any transformation performed by a parser or enrichment service. Separate direct observation from external reputation, derived data, and analyst inference. If a source is absent, stale, or outside coverage, say so.\n\nNo alert and no log are different conditions.\n\n## Reasoning sequence.\n\nEstablish whether the activity succeeded, whether it was authorized, and how far it reached. Compare the affected entity with its own history, peers, approved changes, and business role. Form a theory that is capable of being disproved. Search first around the known time and entity, then expand using durable relationships such as account, device, process ancestry, session, destination, permission, or data flow.\n\nKeep a query journal so another analyst can reproduce the scope.\n\nA common analytical error is equating patch compliance with hardening. Avoid it by naming what each control actually guarantees and by checking adjacent bypass or recovery paths. Choose containment that matches confidence and consequence. A host isolation, account disablement, firewall block, or data-sharing change can interrupt legitimate work and may destroy transient evidence, so follow the incident plan and obtain the required authority.\n\nDefine how the action will be confirmed and reversed.\n\n## Closure and learning.\n\nValidate the original risk, not merely the absence of a repeated alert. Record verified facts, competing explanations considered, scope, action, user or service impact, remaining uncertainty, and the next owner. If the activity is benign, tune narrowly and preserve coverage. If a weakness contributed, create a tracked corrective action with owner, due date, test, and expiration for any exception. This closes the loop between monitoring, response, engineering, and governance.",
          "practical": "Before hardening a live machine, record its purpose and dependencies. Disabling an unfamiliar service without understanding it may create an outage.",
