          "content": "Authentication systems, endpoints, firewalls, DNS, proxies, applications, cloud services, and security tools each record a partial view. Central collection protects logs and makes correlation possible. Time synchronization is essential; otherwise a timeline can put effects before causes. A SIEM normalizes and searches events and runs detection rules. It does not automatically make every alert true. Begin triage by identifying the rule, affected asset and user, timestamp, evidence, expected behavior, related alerts, and business importance.\n\nEnrich cautiously with asset and threat data. Close a false positive only with a reason. Tune noisy logic without hiding real attacks. Escalate when evidence indicates compromise, scope is uncertain, critical assets are involved, or the required action exceeds authority. Record queries and preserve relevant event identifiers. Logs require synchronized time, stable asset and account identifiers, protected collection, reliable parsing, retention, and source-health monitoring.\n\nA missing burst of events or stopped agent is itself useful evidence.\n\nNormalization maps different vendor fields into common concepts but can lose nuance; analysts should open original events for important conclusions. Triage asks whether the detection condition occurred, whether activity was authorized, what asset and user were involved, whether prevention succeeded, what happened before and after, and whether similar entities are affected. A true positive can have no impact because a control blocked it.\n\nA false positive means the target condition was not actually present, not merely that the user had permission.\n\n## Worked case and analyst reasoning\n\nConsider this case: A SIEM alert reports impossible travel for a user who uses a corporate VPN. Start with a neutral statement of what was reported, then locate the original records. Do not let the alert title decide the outcome. Identify the subject, asset, action, outcome, time, source, and affected security property. The concepts that frame this chapter are Log sources, timestamps, normalization, and retention; Rules, correlation, baselines, and false positives; Triage: validity, scope, impact, and escalation.\n\nExplain how each changes the question, the likely impact, or the control boundary.\n\n## Evidence plan.\n\nCollect only what the authorized investigation needs. Relevant evidence includes raw sign-ins, VPN egress, device, token, time, geolocation limits and downstream activity. Record provenance, timestamp and time zone, collection account, source health, retention, and any transformation performed by a parser or enrichment service. Separate direct observation from external reputation, derived data, and analyst inference. If a source is absent, stale, or outside coverage, say so.\n\nNo alert and no log are different conditions.\n\n## Reasoning sequence.\n\nEstablish whether the activity succeeded, whether it was authorized, and how far it reached. Compare the affected entity with its own history, peers, approved changes, and business role. Form a theory that is capable of being disproved. Search first around the known time and entity, then expand using durable relationships such as account, device, process ancestry, session, destination, permission, or data flow.\n\nKeep a query journal so another analyst can reproduce the scope.\n\nA common analytical error is treating enrichment as direct evidence. Avoid it by naming what each control actually guarantees and by checking adjacent bypass or recovery paths. Choose containment that matches confidence and consequence. A host isolation, account disablement, firewall block, or data-sharing change can interrupt legitimate work and may destroy transient evidence, so follow the incident plan and obtain the required authority.\n\nDefine how the action will be confirmed and reversed.\n\n## Closure and learning.\n\nValidate the original risk, not merely the absence of a repeated alert. Record verified facts, competing explanations considered, scope, action, user or service impact, remaining uncertainty, and the next owner. If the activity is benign, tune narrowly and preserve coverage. If a weakness contributed, create a tracked corrective action with owner, due date, test, and expiration for any exception. This closes the loop between monitoring, response, engineering, and governance.",
